Compilation Instructions:
-------------------------
The analysis engine and the host-side CLI are plain JVM modules and need only a
JDK 17+:

    cd AccessAudit
    ./gradlew :core:test          # 79 unit tests over the parsers and rules
    ./gradlew :cli:installDist    # builds cli/build/install/access-audit/bin/access-audit

The Android app additionally needs the Android SDK. The `:app` module is only
included in the build when an SDK is found (via ANDROID_HOME, ANDROID_SDK_ROOT,
or sdk.dir in local.properties), so the two commands above work on a bare JDK:

    ./gradlew :app:assembleDebug
    adb install app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk

Try it without a device:

    ./cli/build/install/access-audit/bin/access-audit \
        --dir samples --sdk 34 --now-ms 1759147920000

Capture from a real device and analyse it:

    tools/adb-access-audit.sh -o capture
    ./cli/build/install/access-audit/bin/access-audit --dir capture --sdk 34


README:
-------
Access Audit logs which app on the phone used which access - read, write,
execute, camera, microphone, location, contacts, SMS - when it did so, whether
the app was visible at the time, and flags the combinations that are worth a
developer's attention.


READ THIS FIRST: what Android actually permits
----------------------------------------------
No app on a stock, unrooted Android device can observe another app's file reads
and writes. There is no API for it, and there is no permission that grants it.
Any app claiming to show you a live read/write/execute log of other apps is
either running with system privileges, requires root, or is lying.

What the platform does keep is a log of *privileged operations* - the "appops"
history behind the Android 12+ Privacy Dashboard. For each app and each
operation it records the last accesses, whether each happened in the foreground
or the background, and how long the app held it. That log is the core of this
tool. Reading it requires GET_APP_OPS_STATS, which is signature|privileged, so
it has to be granted from a shell.

So "read / write / execute" in this app means the *capability class of the
platform operation an app invoked*, not a raw file mode. `core/ops/OpCatalog.kt`
is the table that maps each operation onto a class, and the UI never labels a
static permission as an observed access.

Collector tiers
---------------
Each source of data is a separate collector, and the app shows which are live.
That is deliberate: the difference between "this app used your microphone twice
while you weren't looking" and "this app is allowed to use your microphone" is
the entire value of the report, so the app must never blur the two.

  Tier 0  Static inventory        no permission needed
          Installed packages, requested vs. actually granted permissions,
          install source (store vs. sideload), signing certificate digest,
          target SDK, debuggable/backup flags, declared accessibility and
          device-admin components, native libraries.
          -> what each app *may* do.

  Tier 1  Usage history           Usage access, granted in Settings
          Per-app foreground time. On its own it says nothing about
          permissions; its value is as the denominator for everything else.
          ACTIVE_BUT_NEVER_OPENED refuses to fire without it, because 0ms of
          foreground time with no collector attached means "unknown", not
          "proven".

  Tier 2  Access log              adb or Shizuku   <- the important one
          The real per-app, per-operation access history with foreground/
          background attribution. Two routes:
            - `dumpsys appops` over a privileged shell (preferred: the output
              is parsed by code that has unit tests against captured fixtures);
            - the in-process AppOpsManager API after
                adb shell pm grant dev.accessaudit.app \
                    android.permission.GET_APP_OPS_STATS
              which is faster but only exposes the *last* access per state, so
              it cannot report true totals.

  Tier 3  File-level audit        root only
          SELinux `avc` records from the kernel audit log. This is the only
          source that reports actual filesystem access - and it is mostly a
          record of access that was *denied*, because that is what the kernel
          finds interesting enough to log. It is not a complete I/O trace;
          that needs a custom kernel with fanotify or eBPF. The UI labels it
          accordingly rather than passing it off as a full read/write log.

With only Tier 0 the report is a permission inventory, and the app says so on
every screen instead of implying otherwise.

What gets flagged, and why those rules
--------------------------------------
16 rules, each independent and individually tested. `--rules` lists them. The
ones that carry the most weight:

  ACCESSIBILITY_ABUSE     An accessibility service reads the text of every
                          window and can inject taps. Combined with drawing
                          over other apps it is the standard overlay-phishing
                          toolkit, and the most abused mechanism in Android
                          banking malware.
  ALL_FILES_ACCESS        MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE defeats scoped storage
                          entirely. CRITICAL on a sideloaded app.
  SMS_INTERCEPTION        SMS read access plus network on an app that is not
                          your messaging app - the account-takeover shape,
                          since one-time passcodes arrive by SMS.
  BG_SENSITIVE_ACCESS     Camera, microphone, screen capture or precise
                          location used with no visible UI and no foreground
                          service, so nothing on screen indicated it.
  BG_DATA_HARVEST         Contacts, SMS or all-files access read in the
                          background, which cannot be correlated with any user
                          action.
  ACTIVE_BUT_NEVER_OPENED Sensitive access by an app with essentially no
                          foreground time in the window.
  DYNAMIC_CODE            Loadable code in a directory the app can write to:
                          behaviour that was never in the reviewed APK.
  EXFIL_SHAPE             Sensitive reads actually *observed* on an app with
                          network access. Fires on observation, not on
                          permissions alone.

Two design decisions matter more than the rule list:

**Every finding carries its evidence.** A finding names the observation that
produced it, so a developer can disagree with a rule and still trust the data.
This is why the per-app screen shows the raw access table *above* the findings:
the table is the evidence, the findings are one reading of it.

**Scoring saturates instead of summing.** A plain sum lets a dozen cosmetic
hygiene notes outrank one critical finding, which is exactly how tools like this
end up ignored. Each additional finding counts half as much as the one before,
so a run of findings at one severity converges on twice that severity's weight
and can never reach the next severity up: 20 MEDIUM findings still score below a
single HIGH. `RiskEngineTest` asserts that property directly.

Related: a dangerous permission granted but never used is LOW, not HIGH. It is
unnecessary attack surface, not evidence of misbehaviour, and scoring it higher
would bury the real findings. Suppressions are keyed on package + rule, so
accepting that your maps app uses background location does not also silence its
SMS access.

Output
------
    --format text     worst-first report with evidence (default)
    --format json     full machine-readable report, schemaVersion 1
    --format csv      one row per finding, for triage or diffing scans
    --format matrix   one row per app per operation - the "which app used which
                      access" table, sortable and pivotable
    --package NAME    just that app's access table

Exit codes: 0 nothing flagged, 1 usage/input error, 2 at least one app flagged
(so it can gate CI).

Layout
------
    core/    Analysis engine. Plain JVM, zero dependencies, all the logic worth
             testing: the appops/dumpsys parsers, the operation catalogue, the
             rules, the scoring, the report writers.
    cli/     Host-side analyser for captures taken over adb, no app install.
    app/     Android app: collectors, Room history, Compose UI, periodic scans.
    tools/   adb-access-audit.sh - read-only device capture.
    samples/ A synthetic capture covering a clean app, a store app with
             background location, and a sideloaded app with the spyware shape.

The split is the point: putting the engine in a plain JVM module is what makes
the rules and parsers unit-testable without an emulator, and it means the
on-device report and the host-side report come from identical logic - a rule
cannot behave differently in the two paths.

Privacy
-------
The app does not request android.permission.INTERNET, so nothing it collects can
leave the device unless you export a report and share it deliberately. Backup
and device-transfer are excluded for its database. Both are checkable in
`app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`.

Why periodic scans
------------------
The platform discards appop history after roughly a day. Anything not captured
while it is still there is gone for good, so a WorkManager job captures every
six hours and each scan is stored as a new row. That is what lets the app say
"this app started doing this on Tuesday", which the platform's own 24-hour
window cannot.

Parser robustness
-----------------
`dumpsys appops` output is not a stable API: attribution tags appeared in
Android 11, the flag vocabulary in the `[fg-s]`-style prefix has grown, and some
builds print only a relative age while others also print an absolute timestamp.
The parser is therefore lenient and stateful - it keys off the few line shapes
that have stayed put, ignores indentation, and sends anything it does not
recognise to `unparsedLines` rather than aborting. One new field cannot blank out
a report. Timestamps prefer the relative age, which is timezone-proof.

Known limits
------------
  - Tier 2 counts depend on the route: `dumpsys` gives real totals, the
    in-process API gives only the last access per state.
  - Kernel `avc` records carry monotonic uptime, not wall-clock. Rather than
    inventing a timestamp, Tier 3 events are stored with none.
  - Vendor builds sometimes restrict the shell user, in which case
    `dumpsys appops` returns nothing and the capture script warns about it.
  - The frequency-outlier rule compares against this device's own median, so it
    needs a handful of apps using an operation before it means anything.
  - Nothing here can see an app's network traffic, so EXFIL_SHAPE reports a
    capability and an observation, never a confirmed upload. The wording says so.
